  "content": {
    "title": "Enabling this TSA feature could save you hours in airport security lines",
    "text": "(NEXSTAR) – When I had to catch a flight at Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport on Tuesday morning, the airport’s official website said getting through the main security line would take nearly two hours. I got through in under five minutes. I have a new biometric TSA PreCheck feature to thank for my good fortune as security lines at airports nationwide have grown unbearably long. A partial government shutdown, winter weather and spring break all share the blame from making TSA lines at some airports trail outside the terminal. Even the typically expedited lines for TSA PreCheck or CLEAR – two services you have to enroll in and pay for – have started to grow long. But more than 60 airports have rolled out a new type of PreCheck, called Touchless ID, that can make getting through security even faster. Travelers eligible to use Touchless ID don’t need to pull out a boarding pass or ID. Instead, they walk up to a camera that scans their face and gives them a green light to walk through the security checkpoint. Who can use TSA PreCheck Touchless ID? Not everyone is eligible to use Touchless ID, however. First off, you need to already have TSA PreCheck. (If you don’t, you’ll have to start by applying, getting approved and paying the fee of about $80 for five years of enrollment.) You’ll also need to have a valid passport and upload that information to your airline’s profile (more on how to do this later). Finally, you need to be flying on a participating airline and out of a participating airport. As of March, five major U.S. carriers are participating in Touchless ID: Alaska, American, Delta, Southwest, and United. The Transportation Security Administration says 65 airports will have Touchless ID lines by spring 2026. Major hubs like ATL, DEN, DFW, JFK, LAX and ORD are all up and running. The map below shows the 65 participating airports: Each participating airline has a slightly different list of airports where they’ve started using Touchless ID. Travel website The Points Guy has created a handy list of which airports are active for each of the five participating airlines here. How can I enable Touchless ID? You’ll need to update your airline profile to enable Touchless ID and make sure your boarding pass has the Touchless ID indicator on it. The steps are essentially the same for every airline: Go to your profile on the airline’s app or website Enter your Known Travel Number (KTN) Enter your valid passport number and country Opt in to Touchless ID on your profile For more specific step-by-step instructions, click the links below to see how you can do it with each airline: Alaska Airlines American Airlines Delta Air Lines Southwest Airlines United Airlines What kind of data is collected if I use Touchless ID? According to TSA, the camera at security checkpoints takes a live image of your face and compares it to other photos of you already on record with the government (like your passport photo, driver’s license photo, etc.) to verify your identity. In the past few years, some airlines have also been scanning faces while boarding flights. You may have also had an image taken when entering the country with Customs and Border Patrol. Those images are also added to your biometric profile. When going through the Touchless ID line, TSA collects a live photo of you, your passport number, Known Traveler Number (KTN) and some other data like the time the photo was taken. The agency says the data collected is encrypted and anonymized, analyzed by the Department of Homeland Security, and then deleted within 180 days. You can always opt out of biometric screening, the agency says, and choose standard screening by an officer instead. Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.",
